# dasllama-ladder routes for the dasllama.io vhost in /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.
# This file is the authoritative copy of the service's public boundary — the
# deployed Caddyfile is edited to match it, never the other way round, so a
# route change is reviewable in the same diff as the code it exposes.
#
# Paste inside the `dasllama.io { ... }` block, ahead of `root`/`file_server`.

# The submit routes, and ONLY these, carry the large-body allowance: a record
# store or tune sidecar is an MB-scale JSON document. The service enforces its
# own (smaller) per-kind caps on top — the proxy cap exists because libhv
# buffers a body in full before any handler runs, so this is the only place an
# unbounded chunked POST can be stopped.
#
# The named matcher is required, not stylistic: `handle` takes exactly ONE
# path token (`handle /a /b { }` is a parse error — the playground snippet
# verified this with `caddy validate`).
@ladder_submit path /api/submit/records /api/submit/sidecar
handle @ladder_submit {
	request_body {
		max_size 8MB
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8201
}

# Read-only surface: listings, receipts, sidecar downloads. GET-only handlers,
# but libhv still buffers whatever body arrives — cap it small.
handle /api/versions {
	request_body {
		max_size 64KB
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8201
}
handle /api/runs {
	request_body {
		max_size 64KB
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8201
}
handle /api/submission/* {
	request_body {
		max_size 64KB
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8201
}
handle /api/sidecars {
	request_body {
		max_size 64KB
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8201
}
handle /api/sidecar/* {
	request_body {
		max_size 64KB
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8201
}

# /admin/* and /shutdown are deliberately absent: they are operator surfaces.
# The service also refuses them from a non-loopback peer, so adding a route
# here does not merely expose them — it breaks them.
